// run
// #1472: site (5) of the #1467 family — the arrow-function expression body.
//
// #1467 recorded this site as fixed but shipped it with NO golden, on the
// stated grounds that no shape reaching it would type-check. That reasoning was
// wrong, and the correlation is the lesson: the one site of the five without a
// golden is the one that was still broken.
//
// The shape #1467 tried, `(x: f32): f32 => 4.5`, is not grammatical — §12 gives
// `arrow_p = IDENT [ ":" type ]` with no return-type slot, so BOTH compilers
// reject it with a parser error (E0021). That is a language question, not a bug.
// The GRAMMATICAL shape checks clean and silently returned a wrong value:
//
//     let g = (x: f32) => 4.5
//     g(1.0) == 4.5            // was FALSE
//
// Root cause, and it is a CHECKER bug rather than the lowering hint #1467
// assumed: the arrow fn's result type was taken raw from its body expression,
// so with no expected function type to adapt to, the result stayed the UNTYPED
// constant type `untyped float`. Every other inference site defaults an untyped
// constant before storing it; this one did not. A function type whose result is
// untyped makes the call site and the callee disagree about which ABI slot the
// return arrives in, which is why the f64 form PRINTED 4.5 and COMPARED false.
// Both compilers had it, identically, so no differential could see it — the same
// structural blindness as #1470.
//
// Note what the fix does NOT do: it defaults the body, it does not adapt it to a
// declared result. An arrow passed where `(f32) => f32` is expected still fails
// E0041, because the self-hosted checker carries no expected-type context at all
// (see the note above `checkArrowFn`). That is a separate, LOUD, pre-existing
// gap — both compilers reject it — and it is filed rather than papered over
// here, which is why no such shape appears below.
//
// Every check COMPARES rather than prints, for the reason #1457 taught: this
// family's whole signature is a value that renders plausibly and still compares
// wrong. The `==` results are what get printed. No NaN or -0.0 anywhere — their
// bit patterns differ between x86-64 and AArch64.
//
// A golden case is a single file, so it has no prelude.

// Takes the narrow type explicitly, so the f32 arrow's result is compared as an
// f32 rather than through an untyped literal that could adapt around a defect.
fn isF32Four(x: f32): bool {
  return x == 4.5
}

fn main() {
  // The defect itself: an untyped float literal as the whole body, with no
  // expected function type anywhere to adapt to. The parameter is narrow; the
  // RESULT defaults to f64, which is correct — the bug was that it stayed
  // untyped rather than defaulting at all.
  let g = (x: f32) => 4.5
  print("f32param=${g(1.0) == 4.5}\n")

  // The f64 form is the one that printed 4.5 while comparing false. Its result
  // type IS the untyped default, which is exactly why it looked correct.
  let h = (x: f64) => 4.5
  print("f64param=${h(1.0) == 4.5}\n")

  // Control: the explicit conversion is the path that always worked, and it
  // pins the NARROW result type that the bare literal cannot produce on its own.
  let k = (x: f32) => f32(4.5)
  print("conv=${isF32Four(k(1.0))}\n")

  // Untyped INT bodies travel the same path. This one happened to come out
  // right (its default `i64` is what lowering already assumed), so it is the
  // control proving the fix did not disturb the case that already worked.
  let n = (x: i32) => 5
  print("int=${n(1) == 5}\n")

  // A zero-parameter body, to show the defect was in the RESULT and had nothing
  // to do with the parameter list.
  let z = () => 4.5
  print("noparam=${z() == 4.5}\n")

  // Arithmetic on the returned value, not just equality: a value arriving in
  // the wrong ABI slot fails this even if an equality happened to fall through.
  print("arith=${g(1.0) + h(1.0) == 9.0}\n")
}
